    "q-114357": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114357",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Write $\\frac{x}{x+9}$ in the form $a+\\frac{b}{x+9},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ What is the value of $a+b?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we add and subtract $9$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{x+9}=\\frac{x+9-9}{x+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+9-9}{x+9} & =\\frac{x+9}{x+9}+\\frac{-9}{x+9} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{-9}{x+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=1$ and $b=-9,$ and we get \n\n$$\na+b=1+(-9)=-8.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
