    "q-114380": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114380",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Write $\\frac{9x}{x+2}$ in the form $a+\\frac{b}{x+2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ What is the value of $a+b?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we add and subtract $2$ in parentheses in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9x}{x+2} & =\\frac{9(x+2-2)}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(x+2)+9(-2)}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(x+2)-18}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9(x+2)-18}{x+2} & =\\frac{9(x+2)}{x+2}+\\frac{-18}{x+2} \\\\  & =9+\\frac{-18}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $a=9$ and $b=-18,$ and we get\n\n$$\na+b=9+(-18)=-9.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
